<h3>1) Recommended — the plugin marketplace</h3>
<p>Add the marketplace inside Claude Code, then install the OMC plugin. Run the two lines below one after another in the input box. <strong>Pasting both lines at once fails, so you must run them one line at a time</strong> (stated in the official README).</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Step 1: register the plugin repository (marketplace)</span>
/plugin marketplace add https://github.com/Yeachan-Heo/oh-my-claudecode
<span class="c"># Step 2: install the OMC plugin from the registered repository</span>
/plugin install oh-my-claudecode</code></pre>

<h3>2) Project configuration (--local recommended)</h3>
<p>After installing, per-project configuration is recommended. Adding <code>--local</code> is safe because it does not overwrite your existing global <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Apply the OMC configuration only to the current project — the global configuration is preserved as is</span>
/oh-my-claudecode:omc-setup --local</code></pre>
<p>Use the command below only when you really need a global configuration. However, you should check in advance whether it affects your existing <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Applied commonly to all projects — you need to check whether it affects the existing global CLAUDE.md</span>
/oh-my-claudecode:omc-setup</code></pre>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Recommendation for company dev PCs · shared projects</span>
    Project-local configuration (<code>--local</code>) is recommended over global configuration. It prevents the global <code>CLAUDE.md</code> from getting mixed in when rules differ per project.
</div>

<h3>3) npm installation — the official alternative path for CLI/runtime use</h3>
<p>The plugin approach above is enough for ordinary users, but <strong>npm installation is a separate, officially supported path</strong> as well. Its purpose is different, though. The plugin installation is for using slash commands (<code>/autopilot</code> and so on) inside a Claude Code session, while the npm installation is for using the <code>omc</code> CLI commands (<code>omc doctor</code>, <code>omc team</code>, <code>omc ask</code>, and so on) in a terminal.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Only when you need the terminal CLI (the omc command) — the npm package name is oh-my-claude-sisyphus</span>
npm i -g oh-my-claude-sisyphus@latest</code></pre>
<p>The thing to watch out for is that <strong>the npm package name differs from the repository name</strong>. The project brand is oh-my-claudecode, but the package name published on npm is <code>oh-my-claude-sisyphus</code>. Installing it registers both the <code>oh-my-claudecode</code> and <code>omc</code> commands together. You may see a <code>deprecated prebuild-install</code> warning during installation, but it is a known warning originating from an upstream dependency and does not mean the installation failed. Installation via a curl script is not in the official documentation, so do not use it.</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Installation · updates</span>
    When using the plugin approach, handle installation and updates through Claude Code's plugin management features. If marketplace auto-update is off, first sync the latest version with <code>/plugin marketplace update omc</code>, then run <code>/oh-my-claudecode:omc-setup</code> again to apply the latest configuration.
</div>
